PlayStation pads reach games as XInput pads through SDL, Nintendo pads are mapped by position, XInput rumble lasts until the game changes it, XInput's battery call answers as a wired pad does, and winebus has a virtual test pad.

This repository's patch to winebus and XInput, adapted from neutron's patches/wine/0017-winebus-sdl-gamepads.patch
(github.com/fourlexboehm/neutron at 0ac2574d, LGPL-2.1-or-later, Copyright 2026 the neutron contributors; Wine
11.19 on macOS), rebased on CrossOver 26.3.0's Wine 11.0 with the variables renamed MAVRIK_PAD_*. neutron's
winemac half is patch 0036.

The fault: winebus sees each pad twice on a Mac, through SDL and through IOHID, and keeps one copy. For Sony's
DualShock 4 and DualSense it always kept the raw IOHID copy, and only a gamepad copy gets the XInput child that
XInput and Windows.Gaming.Input open. So a PlayStation pad was no XInput pad: most PC games saw no controller
(mavrik S217 read it in S214's log: "ignoring non-hidraw device 054c:09cc", then "creating hidraw device
054c:09cc"). On Linux, Steam Input hides the real pad behind a virtual Xbox pad; the Mac Steam app makes none for a
Windows game, and mavrik runs no Windows Steam.

- Sony pads keep the SDL copy, so they are XInput gamepads like every other pad, with Sony's ids and Wine's own
  report layout. Raw HID stays Wine's choice when SDL is off or MAVRIK_PAD_HIDRAW=1 is in the Unix environment
  (for a game with its own PlayStation support); services.exe gives winedevice a fresh Windows environment, so
  the SDL bus reads the variable and sets options.pad_hidraw (at the end of struct bus_options). The variable
  counts when the prefix's wineserver starts, as it is winedevice's. Nintendo pads already go through SDL in this
  Wine (11.19 prefers raw HID for the Pro Controller, which neutron's patch covers; a move to that Wine extends
  this to them).
- If the SDL bus can't start, disable_sdl is set, and a failed udev bus sets disable_input, so Wine's existing
  rule keeps the raw IOHID copies (before, they were dropped for SDL copies that never came). The mappings are
  freed whatever disable_sdl says.
- XInput rumble lasts until the game changes it: XInput sends only changes, and SDL stopped the motors after
  Wine's 1000 ms default cutoff. A cutoff of 1000 ms or more becomes SDL's 0 (no end); a shorter one a game set
  still applies.
- XInputGetBatteryInformation answers a connected pad as Windows does for a wired one (BATTERY_TYPE_WIRED,
  BATTERY_LEVEL_FULL; a headset query reads disconnected) instead of ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED, which no Windows driver
  returns.
- SDL may deliver pad events to winedevice, which never has the focus
  (SDL_HINT_JOYSTICK_ALLOW_BACKGROUND_EVENTS; SDL3 also turns on GameController's background monitoring with it,
  for pads it reads through GameController). An environment variable of the same name still wins.
- Nintendo pads are mapped by position, not by their printed labels (SDL_HINT_GAMECONTROLLER_USE_BUTTON_LABELS
  "0", SDL3's own default): the Switch Pro Controller's right face button is XInput's B and its bottom one XInput's
  A, where an Xbox pad has them, so a game's "press A" prompt means the same place on every pad (mavrik's choice,
  S217; Steam Input on Windows offers both). SDL2's default had matched labels. The variable still wins.
- MAVRIK_PAD_VIRTUAL=1 (Xbox 360 ids) or =<vid>:<pid> attaches an SDL virtual gamepad that presses A, then B and
  dpad up, then moves the left stick and trigger, every 400 ms; it echoes rumble back as the right stick and
  trigger, and a rumble of 0xffff/0x0001 unplugs it for one second. It stays still for 1.5 s after each plug-in:
  winebus drops input until hidclass starts the device, and moves the dpad's hat switch by steps, so a dpad press
  lost that way and its release left the hat stuck at "down" (mavrik S217, 4 of 13 probe runs before the hold;
  Wine's own behaviour, not this patch's). With it, mavrik's tools/gamepadprobe.c (from
  neutron's tests/gamepad_list.c) checks the whole path with no real pad. Off unless the variable is set.

Checked (mavrik S217 lab): applies after patches 0001 to 0034 with no fuzz; winebus and the xinput DLLs build with
the engine's toolchain with no new warning. On r10 with these modules laid in (display driver off, a fresh prefix),
tools/gamepadprobe.c's selftest passed 18 of 18 checks in 64- and 32-bit, in two passes, with the virtual pad as
045e:028e (Xbox 360), 054c:09cc (DualShock 4), 054c:0ce6 (DualSense) and 057e:2009 (Switch Pro): XInput buttons
and axes, rumble echoed and still held after 1.5 s, the stop, the wired battery, DirectInput, raw input,
Windows.Gaming.Input, unplug and replug. With MAVRIK_PAD_HIDRAW=1 the Sony ids' SDL copies were dropped ("ignoring
non-hidraw device") and the Xbox id still passed 18 of 18. Before the positional hint, 057e:2009 passed 15 or 16 of
18 (the scripted bottom button read as B), and 18 of 18 only with SDL_GAMECONTROLLER_USE_BUTTON_LABELS=0 set by
hand. scripts/gates.py's controllers gate runs these cases. Real pads (SDL's HIDAPI and GameController drivers
inside winedevice, real motors) are for mavrik's walk.

